<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chennakeshava Temple, Belur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1117</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chennakeshava temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, consecrated in 1117 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a>. Built of soapstone on a star-shaped platform, it is covered with finely carved friezes, bracket figures and sculpture. It is one of the finest examples of Hoysala architecture and part of a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chennakeshava-temple-belur.jpg" alt="Chennakeshava Temple, Belur" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bikashrd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Courtyard_of_Chennakesava_Temple_-_Belur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
